course: dictionary
lesson: 7765
title: One word, four meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The words around it, its context, tell you which meaning is right. «Get out, you thief, that's my purse!» Here a purse is something a thief can steal, so it must be a small bag for money.
explain: Now look at «It is a mermaid's purse, an egg case that once held a baby shark or ray.» This purse has nothing to do with money. The clues are egg case and baby shark, so this is a different meaning.
explain: Always check by swapping your meaning into the sentence. «Harry also took a pound from Mum's purse for a lolly» still makes sense if purse means a small bag for money. A purse can also be a prize, as in a race with a big purse, but that meaning does not fit Harry's sentence.
fact: A purse is a small bag for carrying money and coins, used in the UK, Ireland and Australia.
fact: In American English, a purse is a woman's handbag.
fact: In a race or contest, the purse is the money given as the prize, as in «the race had a big purse».
fact: To purse your lips is to squeeze them into a small round shape, as in «she pursed her lips».
fact: A mermaid's purse is an egg case that once held a baby shark or ray.
exercise: «Get out, you thief, that's my purse!» What is a purse in this sentence? | A prize for winning a race | To squeeze your lips shut | A mermaid's egg case | *A small bag for carrying money
exercise: «It is a mermaid's purse, an egg case that once held a baby shark or ray.» What does purse mean here? | *An egg case that once held a baby shark or ray | A bag for money | The prize in a race | A way of squeezing the lips
exercise: In «the race had a big purse», what is the purse? | A bag for carrying coins | A woman's handbag | A small round shape | *The money prize for the winner
test: Which meaning fits «Harry also took a pound from Mum's purse for a lolly»? | An egg case | To squeeze your lips | A prize for a race | *A small bag for carrying money
test: «She pursed her lips and said nothing.» What does pursed mean here? | Ran away quickly | *Squeezed into a small round shape | Shouted loudly | Won a prize
exam: In American English, what does purse mean? | The prize money in a race | A shark's egg case | *A woman's handbag | A small bag for coins
